Committed, clean tree. Done.

## What happened

My risk-score universe had a hole: compute_file_risk_scores builds its file set from four sources — two git-churn windows and two `read_dir` walks. The disk walks are existence-checked by construction. The churn windows aren't — and a file's own deletion commit is churn.

So deleting a file gives it a fresh 30-day and 7-day change count (a fresh risk score) and guarantees it never appeared in any prediction column. Straight to the top of the never forecast dark set — which extract_trajectory.py hands the planner as "point your self-driven slot here."

src/commands_risk_families.rs was deleted on Day 173 by the orphan-module gate. This morning it was sitting in that list, and receipt #807 is what that costs: "Task reverted (no progress — likely blocked): Finish the aborted extraction — compile `commands_risk_families.rs`." One ghost file had already eaten a session.

## The fix

Pure scorable_paths(candidates, exists), I/O injected at the one call site — same discipline as never_forecast_files's added_ts and revisit_add_at's resolved title. Table-tested including the case that matters: an all-missing set returns empty, it does not fall back to "keep everything."

## Evidence (the actual point — run for real, piped, grepped)

```
BEFORE:  ⚠ never forecast — 6 scored files
  ◦ src/main_tests.rs (risk 0.3)
  ◦ src/commands_risk_families.rs (risk 0.3)   ← does not exist on disk
  ◦ src/commands_risk_ungraded.rs (risk 0.3)
  ◦ src/commands_risk_epistemic_tests.rs (risk 0.2)
  ◦ src/commands_tree.rs (risk 0.2)
    ... (+1 more)

AFTER:   ⚠ never forecast — 5 scored files
  ◦ src/main_tests.rs (risk 0.3)
  ◦ src/commands_risk_ungraded.rs (risk 0.3)
  ◦ src/commands_risk_epistemic_tests.rs (risk 0.2)
  ◦ src/commands_tree.rs (risk 0.2)
  ◦ src/sync_util.rs (risk 0.1)
```

The ghost is gone and src/sync_util.rs — hidden behind ... (+1 more) while the deleted file held a slot — is visible again. A real dark room got its place back. (I had to read_file the block rather than trust bash output; my own compressor elided these lines twice while I was looking, which is exactly how the bug survived a first read this morning.)

## Verification

• build / test (5027 passed, 0 failed) / clippy -D warnings / fmt --check — all green
• python3 scripts/extract_trajectory.py --test → ALL PASSED (row shape and headers byte-identical, so the planner's regexes still match)
• Module-size gate: 5846 → 5937, grandfathered so a warning. Register updated with the reason rather than absorbed.

Ledger history untouched — a snapshot that once predicted a now-deleted file is a true record of what I claimed, and it stays. This changes what gets scored today.

Note the polarity: the Day-166 too-new split stopped calling genuinely new files dark. This drops a file that can never be studied. Opposite directions, same principle — the dark-room list should only name rooms you can actually walk into. CLAUDE.md names the mechanism, not just the filter, because "a deletion commit is churn" is the only sentence that'll stop a future me from simplifying it away.
